#. TRANSLATORS: MDX-derived text from packages/learn/tutorial.mdx
msgid "The 6502 uses a 16-bit address bus, meaning that there are 65536 bytes of memory available to the processor. Remember that a byte is represented by two hex characters, so the memory locations are generally represented as <tt>$0000 - $ffff</tt>. There are various ways to refer to these memory locations, as detailed below."
msgstr "Le 6502 usa un bus de adresse de 16-bit, significante que il ha 65536 bytes de memoria disponibile al processor. Recorda que un byte es representate per duo characteres hex, assi le locationes de memoria es generalmente representate como <tt>$0000 - $ffff</tt>. Il ha varie manieras de referer a iste locationes de memoria, como detaliate infra."

#. TRANSLATORS: MDX-derived text from packages/learn/tutorial.mdx
msgid "The <tt>BIT</tt> opcode is similar to <tt>AND</tt>, but the calculation is only used to set the zero flag - the actual result is discarded. The zero flag is set only if the result of AND-ing the accumulator with argument is zero. When we're looking at powers of two, the zero flag will only be set if the two numbers are not the same. For example, <tt>0001 AND 0001</tt> is not zero, but <tt>0001 AND 0010</tt> is zero."
msgstr "Le opcode <tt>BIT</tt> es simile a <tt>AND</tt>, ma le calculation es solo usate pro definir le bandiera de zero - le resultato actual es discardate. Le bandiera de zero es definite solo si le resultato de AND-ing le accumulator con argumento es zero. Quando nos reguarda potentias de duo, le bandiera de zero sera solo definite si le duo numeros non es le mesme. Pro exemplo, <tt>0001 AND 0001</tt> non es zero, ma <tt>0001 AND 0010</tt> es zero."

#. TRANSLATORS: MDX-derived text from packages/learn/tutorial.mdx
msgid "The <tt>init</tt> subroutine defers to two subroutines, <tt>initSnake</tt> and <tt>generateApplePosition</tt>. <tt>initSnake</tt> sets the snake direction, length, and then loads the initial memory locations of the snake head and body. The byte pair at <tt>$10</tt> contains the screen location of the head, the pair at <tt>$12</tt> contains the location of the single body segment, and <tt>$14</tt> contains the location of the tail (the tail is the last segment of the body and is drawn in black to keep the snake moving). This happens in the following code:"
msgstr "Le subrutina <tt>init</tt> differe a duo subrutinas, <tt>initSnake</tt> e <tt>generateApplePosition</tt>. <tt>initSnake</tt> defini le direction del serpente, longitude, e pois carga le locationes initial de memoria del capite del serpente e corpore. Le par de bytes a <tt>$10</tt> contine le location de schermo del capite, le par a <tt>$12</tt> contine le location del sol segmento del corpore, e <tt>$14</tt> contine le location del cauda (le cauda es le ultime segmento del corpore e es designate in negro pro mantener le serpente movente). Iste occurre in le codice sequente:"

#. TRANSLATORS: MDX-derived text from packages/learn/tutorial.mdx
msgid "The <tt>loop</tt> subroutine itself just calls a number of subroutines sequentially, before looping back on itself:"
msgstr "Le subrutina <tt>loop</tt> mesme solo appella un numero de subrutinas sequentemente, ante de iterar retro sur se mesme:"

#. TRANSLATORS: Detailed description of Accumulator register
msgid "The Accumulator (A) is the primary register for arithmetic and logic operations. All basic calculations like ADD, SUBTRACT, AND, OR, etc. use this register. It's the most frequently used register in 6502 assembly programming."
msgstr "Le Accumulator (A) es le registro primari pro operationes arithmetic e logic. Tote calculos basic como ADDER, SUBTRAHER, AND, OR, etc. usa iste registro. Illo es le registro plus frequentemente usate in programmation de assemblage 6502."

#. TRANSLATORS: Detailed description of Program Counter register
msgid "The Program Counter contains the memory address of the next instruction to execute. It automatically increments after each instruction and can be modified by jumps, branches, calls, and returns. Essential for program flow control and debugging."
msgstr "Le Contator de programma contine le adresse de memoria del proxime instruction a executar. Illo automaticamente incrementa post cata instruction e pote esser modificate per saltos, ramificationes, appellos, e retornos. Essential pro controlo de fluxo de programma e debugging."

#. TRANSLATORS: Detailed description of Stack Pointer register
msgid "The Stack Pointer manages the call stack for subroutines and interrupts. It starts at $FF (top of stack) and grows downward. Used for PUSH/PULL operations, JSR/RTS calls, and interrupt handling. Critical for nested function calls."
msgstr "Le Punctator de pila gere le pila de appella pro subrutinas e interruptiones. Illo comencia a $FF (cima de pila) e cresce bassum. Usate pro operationes PUSH/PULL, appellos JSR/RTS, e maneamento de interruptiones. Critico pro appellos de functiones nidate."

#. TRANSLATORS: Detailed description of X Index register
msgid "The X Index Register is used for indexed addressing modes and counting operations. It can hold loop counters, array indices, and is essential for accessing memory locations relative to a base address using X as an offset. Special: TSX/TXS transfer X to/from the stack pointer."
msgstr "Le Registro de indice X es usate pro modos de adressage indexate e operationes de contation. Illo pote mantener contadores de loop, indices de array, e es essential pro acceder locationes de memoria relative a un adresse base usante X como distantia. Special: TSX/TXS transfer X a/de le punctator de pila."

#. TRANSLATORS: Detailed description of Y Index register
msgid "The Y Index Register is similar to X and has no special functions. Commonly used for indexed addressing and data movement."
msgstr "Le Registro de indice Y es simile a X e ha nulle functiones special. Communmente usate pro adressage indexate e movimento de datos."

#. TRANSLATORS: MDX-derived text from packages/learn/tutorial.mdx
msgid "The black game console screen now should show three coloured \"pixels\" at the top left."
msgstr "Le schermo nigre del consola de joco ora deberea monstrar tres \"pixeles\" colorate in alto a sinistra."

#. TRANSLATORS: MDX-derived text from packages/learn/tutorial.mdx
msgid "The code block below contains the entire source code of the game. I'll explain how it works in the following sections."
msgstr "Le bloco de codice infra contine tote le codice fonte del joco. Io explicara como illo functiona in le sectiones sequente."

#. TRANSLATORS: MDX-derived text from packages/learn/tutorial.mdx
msgid "The effect of this is to mask out the least significant two bits of the accumulator, setting the others to zero. This converts a number in the range of 0–255 to a number in the range of 0–3."
msgstr "Le effecto de isto es mascar le duo bits minus significante del accumulator, definiente le alteres a zero. Iste converte un numero in le rango de 0–255 a un numero in le rango de 0–3."

#. TRANSLATORS: MDX-derived text from packages/learn/tutorial.mdx
msgid "The first instruction causes execution to jump to the <tt>init</tt> label. This sets <tt>X</tt>, then returns to the next instruction, <tt>JSR loop</tt>. This jumps to the <tt>loop</tt> label, which increments <tt>X</tt> until it is equal to <tt>$05</tt>. After that we return to the next instruction, <tt>JSR end</tt>, which jumps to the end of the file. This illustrates how <tt>JSR</tt> and <tt>RTS</tt> can be used together to create modular code."
msgstr "Le prime instruction causa que execution salta al etiquetta <tt>init</tt>. Iste defini <tt>X</tt>, pois retorna al proxime instruction, <tt>JSR loop</tt>. Iste salta al etiquetta <tt>loop</tt>, que incrementa <tt>X</tt> usque illo es equal a <tt>$05</tt>. Post illo nos retorna al proxime instruction, <tt>JSR end</tt>, que salta al fin del file. Iste illustra como <tt>JSR</tt> e <tt>RTS</tt> pote esser usate insimul pro crear codice modular."

#. TRANSLATORS: MDX-derived text from packages/learn/tutorial.mdx
msgid "The first line shows the <tt>A</tt>, <tt>X</tt> and <tt>Y</tt> registers (<tt>A</tt> is often called the \"accumulator\"). Each register holds a single byte. Most operations work on the contents of these registers."
msgstr "Le prime linea monstra le registros <tt>A</tt>, <tt>X</tt> e <tt>Y</tt> (<tt>A</tt> es frequentemente appellate le \"accumulator\"). Cata registro mantene un sol byte. Le majoritate del operationes functiona sur le contento de iste registros."

#. TRANSLATORS: MDX-derived text from packages/learn/tutorial.mdx
msgid "The first subroutine, <tt>readKeys</tt>, takes the job of accepting user input. The memory location <tt>$ff</tt> holds the ascii code of the most recent key press in this simulator. The value is loaded into the accumulator, then compared to <tt>$77</tt> (the hex code for W), <tt>$64</tt> (D), <tt>$73</tt> (S) and <tt>$61</tt> (A). If any of these comparisons are successful, the program branches to the appropriate section."
msgstr "Le prime subrutina, <tt>readKeys</tt>, prende le labor de acceptar entrata de usator. Le location de memoria <tt>$ff</tt> mantene le codice ascii del plus recente clave pressate in iste simulator. Le valor es cargate in le accumulator, pois comparate a <tt>$77</tt> (le codice hex pro W), <tt>$64</tt> (D), <tt>$73</tt> (S) e <tt>$61</tt> (A). Si qualcunque de iste comparationes es successose, le programma ramifica al section appropriate."

#. TRANSLATORS: MDX-derived text from packages/learn/tutorial.mdx
msgid "The first two bytes hold the location of the apple. This is updated every time the snake eats the apple. Byte <tt>$02</tt> contains the current direction. <tt>1</tt> means up, <tt>2</tt> right, <tt>4</tt> down, and <tt>8</tt> left. The reasoning behind these numbers will become clear later."
msgstr "Le prime duo bytes mantene le location del pomo. Iste es actualisate cata vice que le serpente mangia le pomo. Byte <tt>$02</tt> contine le direction currente. <tt>1</tt> significa sursum, <tt>2</tt> dextra, <tt>4</tt> bassum, e <tt>8</tt> sinistra. Le rationamento detra iste numeros devenira clar plus tarde."

#. TRANSLATORS: MDX-derived text from packages/learn/tutorial.mdx
msgid "The game loop"
msgstr "Le loop del joco"

#. TRANSLATORS: MDX-derived text from packages/learn/tutorial.mdx
msgid "The hex should look something like this:"
msgstr "Le hex deberea reguardar alcuno cosa como isto:"

#. TRANSLATORS: MDX-derived text from packages/learn/tutorial.mdx
msgid "The hex value <tt>$03</tt> is represented in binary as <tt>00000011</tt>. The <tt>AND</tt> opcode performs a bitwise AND of the argument with the accumulator. For example, if the accumulator contains the binary value <tt>10101010</tt>, then the result of <tt>AND</tt> with <tt>00000011</tt> will be <tt>00000010</tt>."
msgstr "Le valor hex <tt>$03</tt> es representate in binari como <tt>00000011</tt>. Le opcode <tt>AND</tt> executa un AND bitwise del argumento con le accumulator. Pro exemplo, si le accumulator contine le valor binari <tt>10101010</tt>, pois le resultato de <tt>AND</tt> con <tt>00000011</tt> sera <tt>00000010</tt>."

#. TRANSLATORS: MDX-derived text from packages/learn/tutorial.mdx
msgid "The last section shows the processor flags. Each flag is one bit, so all seven flags live in a single byte. The flags are set by the processor to give information about the previous instruction. More on that later. <a href=\"https://web.archive.org/web/20210626024532/http://www.obelisk.me.uk/6502/registers.html\">Read more about the registers and flags here</a>."
msgstr "Le ultime section monstra le bandieras del processor. Cata bandiera es un bit, assi tote le septe bandieras vive in un sol byte. Le bandieras es definite per le processor pro dar information a proposito del instruction previe. Plus a proposito de illo plus tarde. <a href=\"https://web.archive.org/web/20210626024532/http://www.obelisk.me.uk/6502/registers.html\">Lege plus a proposito del registros e bandieras hic</a>."

#. TRANSLATORS: MDX-derived text from packages/learn/tutorial.mdx
msgid "The last subroutine, <tt>spinWheels</tt>, is just there because the game would run too fast otherwise. All <tt>spinWheels</tt> does is count <tt>X</tt> down from zero until it hits zero again. The first <tt>dex</tt> wraps, making <tt>X</tt> <tt>#$ff</tt>."
msgstr "Le ultime subrutina, <tt>spinWheels</tt>, es justo ibi proque le joco currerea troppo rapide alteremente. Tote que <tt>spinWheels</tt> face es contar <tt>X</tt> bassum de zero usque illo colide zero de novo. Le prime <tt>dex</tt> se involve, faciente <tt>X</tt> <tt>#$ff</tt>."

#. TRANSLATORS: MDX-derived text from packages/learn/tutorial.mdx
msgid "The length is initialized to <tt>4</tt>, so <tt>X</tt> starts off as <tt>3</tt>. <tt>LDA $10,x</tt> loads the value of <tt>$13</tt> into <tt>A</tt>, then <tt>STA $12,x</tt> stores this value into <tt>$15</tt>. <tt>X</tt> is decremented, and we loop. Now <tt>X</tt> is <tt>2</tt>, so we load <tt>$12</tt> and store it into <tt>$14</tt>. This loops while <tt>X</tt> is positive (<tt>BPL</tt> means \"branch if positive\")."
msgstr "Le longitude es initialisate a <tt>4</tt>, assi <tt>X</tt> comencia como <tt>3</tt>. <tt>LDA $10,x</tt> carga le valor de <tt>$13</tt> in <tt>A</tt>, pois <tt>STA $12,x</tt> immagazina iste valor in <tt>$15</tt>. <tt>X</tt> es decrementate, e nos itera. Ora <tt>X</tt> es <tt>2</tt>, assi nos carga <tt>$12</tt> e immagazina lo in <tt>$14</tt>. Iste itera durante que <tt>X</tt> es positive (<tt>BPL</tt> significa \"ramificar si positive\")."

#. TRANSLATORS: MDX-derived text from packages/learn/tutorial.mdx
msgid "The next bit updates the head of the snake depending on the direction. This is probably the most complicated part of the code, and it's all reliant on how memory locations map to the screen, so let's look at that in more detail."
msgstr "Le proxime bit actualisa le capite del serpente dependente del direction. Iste es probabilmente le parte plus complicate del codice, e illo es tote dependente de como locationes de memoria se mappa al schermo, assi lassiamo reguardar illo in plus detalio."

#. TRANSLATORS: MDX-derived text from packages/learn/tutorial.mdx
msgid "The next line, <tt>BNE decrement</tt>, will shift execution to the decrement label if the <tt>Z</tt> flag is set to <tt>0</tt> (meaning that the two values in the <tt>CPX</tt> comparison were not equal), otherwise it does nothing and we store <tt>X</tt> to <tt>$0201</tt>, then finish the program."
msgstr "Le proxime linea, <tt>BNE decrement</tt>, cambiara execution al etiquetta decrement si le bandiera <tt>Z</tt> es definite a <tt>0</tt> (significante que le duo valores in le comparation <tt>CPX</tt> non esseva equal), alteremente illo non face nihil e nos immagazina <tt>X</tt> a <tt>$0201</tt>, pois termina le programma."

#. TRANSLATORS: MDX-derived text from packages/learn/tutorial.mdx
msgid "The next subroutine, <tt>checkCollision</tt>, defers to <tt>checkAppleCollision</tt> and <tt>checkSnakeCollision</tt>. <tt>checkAppleCollision</tt> just checks to see if the two bytes holding the location of the apple match the two bytes holding the location of the head. If they do, the length is increased and a new apple position is generated."
msgstr "Le proxime subrutina, <tt>checkCollision</tt>, differe a <tt>checkAppleCollision</tt> e <tt>checkSnakeCollision</tt>. <tt>checkAppleCollision</tt> solo verifica si le duo bytes mantenente le location del pomo coincide con le duo bytes mantenente le location del capite. Si illes face, le longitude es augmentate e un nove position de pomo es generate."

#. TRANSLATORS: MDX-derived text from packages/learn/tutorial.mdx
msgid "The next subroutine, <tt>generateApplePosition</tt>, sets the apple location to a random position on the display. First, it loads a random byte into the accumulator (<tt>$fe</tt> is a random number generator in this simulator). This is stored into <tt>$00</tt>. Next, a different random byte is loaded into the accumulator, which is then <tt>AND</tt>-ed with the value <tt>$03</tt>. This part requires a bit of a detour."
msgstr "Le proxime subrutina, <tt>generateApplePosition</tt>, defini le location del pomo a un position casual sur le schermo. Prime, illo carga un byte casual in le accumulator (<tt>$fe</tt> es un generator de numeros casual in iste simulator). Iste es immagazinate in <tt>$00</tt>. Sequente, un differente byte casual es cargate in le accumulator, que es pois <tt>AND</tt>-ed con le valor <tt>$03</tt>. Iste parte require un micre detour."

#. TRANSLATORS: MDX-derived text from packages/learn/tutorial.mdx
msgid "The opposite of <tt>ADC</tt> is <tt>SBC</tt> (subtract with carry). Write a program that uses this instruction."
msgstr "Le opposite de <tt>ADC</tt> es <tt>SBC</tt> (subtraher con reporto). Scribe un programma que usa iste instruction."

#. TRANSLATORS: MDX-derived text from packages/learn/tutorial.mdx
msgid "The opposite of <tt>BNE</tt> is <tt>BEQ</tt>. Try writing a program that uses <tt>BEQ</tt>."
msgstr "Le opposite de <tt>BNE</tt> es <tt>BEQ</tt>. Tenta scriber un programma que usa <tt>BEQ</tt>."

#. TRANSLATORS: MDX-derived text from packages/learn/tutorial.mdx
msgid "The problem is, <tt>$184</tt> is too big to fit in a single byte (the max is <tt>$FF</tt>), and the registers can only hold a single byte. It's OK though; the processor isn't actually dumb. If you were looking carefully enough, you'll have noticed that the carry flag was set to <tt>1</tt> after this operation. So that's how you know."
msgstr "Le problema es, <tt>$184</tt> es troppo grande pro caber in un sol byte (le maximo es <tt>$FF</tt>), e le registros pote solo mantener un sol byte. Illo es OK tamen; le processor non es actualmente stupide. Si tu reguardava bastante attentemente, tu habera notate que le bandiera de reporto esseva definite a <tt>1</tt> post iste operation. Assi es como tu sape."

#. TRANSLATORS: MDX-derived text from packages/learn/tutorial.mdx
msgid "The processor knows where to return to because <tt>JSR</tt> pushes the address minus one of the next instruction onto the stack before jumping to the given location. <tt>RTS</tt> pops this location, adds one to it, and jumps to that location. An example:"
msgstr "Le processor sape ubi retornar proque <tt>JSR</tt> pusha le adresse minus un del proxime instruction sur le pila ante saltar al location date. <tt>RTS</tt> poppa iste location, adde un a illo, e salta a ille location. Un exemplo:"

#. TRANSLATORS: MDX-derived text from packages/learn/tutorial.mdx
msgid "The result of this subroutine is to load a random byte into <tt>$00</tt>, and a random number between 2 and 5 into <tt>$01</tt>. Because the least significant byte comes first with indirect addressing, this translates into a memory address between <tt>$0200</tt> and <tt>$05ff</tt>: the exact range used to draw the display."
msgstr "Le resultato de iste subrutina es cargar un byte casual in <tt>$00</tt>, e un numero casual inter 2 e 5 in <tt>$01</tt>. Proque le byte minus significante veni prime con adressage indirecte, iste se traduce in un adresse de memoria inter <tt>$0200</tt> e <tt>$05ff</tt>: le exacte rango usate pro designar le schermo."

#. TRANSLATORS: Example description for the Snake game
msgid "The snake game from the tutorial"
msgstr "Le joco Snake del tutorial"

#. TRANSLATORS: MDX-derived text from packages/learn/tutorial.mdx
msgid "The stack"
msgstr "Le pila"

#. TRANSLATORS: MDX-derived text from packages/learn/tutorial.mdx
msgid "The stack in a 6502 processor is just like any other stack - values are pushed onto it and popped (\"pulled\" in 6502 parlance) off it. The current depth of the stack is measured by the stack pointer, a special register. The stack lives in memory between <tt>$0100</tt> and <tt>$01ff</tt>. The stack pointer is initially <tt>$ff</tt>, which points to memory location <tt>$01ff</tt>. When a byte is pushed onto the stack, the stack pointer becomes <tt>$fe</tt>, or memory location <tt>$01fe</tt>, and so on."
msgstr "Le pila in un processor 6502 es justo como qualcunque altere pila - valores es pushate sur illo e poppate (\"pullate\" in parlar 6502) del illo. Le profunditate currente del pila es mesurate per le punctator de pila, un registro special. Le pila vive in memoria inter <tt>$0100</tt> e <tt>$01ff</tt>. Le punctator de pila es initialmente <tt>$ff</tt>, que punta a location de memoria <tt>$01ff</tt>. Quando un byte es pushate sur le pila, le punctator de pila deveni <tt>$fe</tt>, o location de memoria <tt>$01fe</tt>, e assi via."

#. TRANSLATORS: MDX-derived text from packages/learn/tutorial.mdx
msgid "The zero page of memory is used to store a number of game state variables, as noted in the comment block at the top of the game. Everything in <tt>$00</tt>, <tt>$01</tt> and <tt>$10</tt> upwards is a pair of bytes representing a two-byte memory location that will be looked up using indirect addressing. These memory locations will all be between <tt>$0200</tt> and <tt>$05ff</tt> - the section of memory corresponding to the game console. For example, if <tt>$00</tt> and <tt>$01</tt> contained the values <tt>$01</tt> and <tt>$02</tt>, they would be referring to the second pixel of the display ( <tt>$0201</tt> - remember, the least significant byte comes first in indirect addressing)."
msgstr "Le pagina zero de memoria es usate pro immagazinar un numero de variabiles de stato de joco, como notate in le bloco de commentos al initio del joco. Toto in <tt>$00</tt>, <tt>$01</tt> e <tt>$10</tt> in avante es un par de bytes representante un location de memoria de duo bytes que sera recercate usante adressage indirecte. Iste locationes de memoria sera tote inter <tt>$0200</tt> e <tt>$05ff</tt> - le section de memoria correspondente al consola de joco. Pro exemplo, si <tt>$00</tt> e <tt>$01</tt> contineva le valores <tt>$01</tt> e <tt>$02</tt>, illes refererea al secunde pixel del schermo ( <tt>$0201</tt> - recorda, le byte minus significante veni prime in adressage indirecte)."

#. TRANSLATORS: MDX-derived text from packages/learn/tutorial.mdx
msgid "Then why 6502? Why not a <i>useful</i> assembly language, like <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/X86\">x86</a>? Well, I don't think learning x86 is useful. I don't think you'll ever have to <i>write</i> assembly language in your day job - for some people this is an academic exercise, something to expand your mind and your thinking. 6502 was originally written in a different age, a time when the majority of developers were writing assembly directly, rather than in these new-fangled high-level programming languages. So, it was designed to be written by humans. More modern assembly languages are meant to written by compilers, so let's leave it to them. Plus, 6502 is <i>fun</i>. Nobody ever called x86 <i>fun</i>."
msgstr "Alora proque 6502? Proque non un lingua de assemblage <i>utile</i>, como <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/X86\">x86</a>? Ben, io non pensa que apprender x86 es utile. Io non pensa que tu jammais habera besonio de <i>scriber</i> lingua de assemblage in tu labor diurne - pro alcun personas isto es un exercitio academic, alcun cosa pro expander tu mente e tu pensata. 6502 esseva originalmente scribite in un etate differente, un tempore quando le majoritate de disveloppatores scribeva assemblage directemente, plus tosto que in iste nove linguas de programmation de alte nivello. Assi, illo esseva designate pro esser scribite per humanos. Plus moderne linguas de assemblage es designate pro esser scribite per compilatores, assi lassiamo lo a illes. Plus, 6502 es <i>divertente</i>. Nemo jammais appella x86 <i>divertente</i>."

#. TRANSLATORS: MDX-derived text from packages/learn/tutorial.mdx
msgid "There's also a very practical reason: retro game development. The homebrew scene is alive and well—new games are still <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1KLhFNmmgUo\">released for the NES</a> (some even as physical cartridges) and for many other classic systems; the NES is just an example. Even if your target is something like the SNES, starting with 6502 pays off: the SNES CPU (65C816) is backward-compatible with the 6502, so the fundamentals you learn here—registers, addressing modes, and the core instruction set—carry straight over."
msgstr "Il ha tamben un rationamento multo practic: disveloppamento de jocos retro. Le scena homebrew es vive e ben—nove jocos es ancora <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1KLhFNmmgUo\">liberate pro le NES</a> (alcun mesmo como cartuchos physic) e pro multe altere systemas classic; le NES es justo un exemplo. Mesmo si tu objectivo es alcun cosa como le SNES, comenciar con 6502 paga: le CPU SNES (65C816) es retro-compatibile con le 6502, assi le fundamentos que tu apprende hic—registros, modos de adressage, e le collection de instructiones core—se porta directemente."

#. TRANSLATORS: MDX-derived text from packages/learn/tutorial.mdx
